d that too much  centralized power would lead to  governmental tyranny  these attitudes shaped both parties  reactions to the newspapers of the day  late 18th century newspapers could be as  vicious and unscrupulous as some of our  media and Democratic Republican  newspapers were merciless in their  attacks on President Adams and other  Federalist leaders  some of these attacks were blatant lies  and Adams believed correctly that his  own vice president Thomas Jefferson was  behind some of them  the Federalists saw these attacks as  more than annoyances  they believed that the common man was  easily manipulated and worried that  libelist newspaper attacks on the  Federalist leadership would lead the  country down the road to chaos  just like many today complain about  disinformation President Adams moaned  that the Jeffersonian press would go to  all lengths of profligacy falsehood and  malignity in defaming our government  to respond to these dangers Federalists  in Congress proposed the Alien and  Sedition Acts of 1798.  we'll focus on the Sedition Act which  permitted the criminal prosecution of  any person who writes or utters  any false scandalous and malicious  writing against the government or either  House of the Congress or the president  of the United States with intent to  defame or to bring them into contempt or  disrepute  The Proposal prompted great debates  which broke down along party lines  Democratic Republican opponents of the  bill argued that it violated the First  Amendment which provided that Congress  shall make no law abridging the freedom  of speech or of the press  surely a law that punished a person for  criticizing the government ran afoul of  that Amendment  moreover Democratic Republicans  contended that Federalists wanted the  law to serve improper partisan ends  Pennsylvania Congressman Albert Gallatin  argued that the law represented the  Federalists unconstitutional effort to  perpetuate their Authority  Federalists responded that 