ring  World War One in June 1917 two months  after the U.S entered the war against  Germany Congress passed the Espionage  Act among other things the ACT  prohibited speech that incited military  insubordination or the refusal to serve  in the armed forces  the next year Congress passed amendments  to the ACT criminalizing speech with the  intent to cause scorn for the U.S  government to stop the production of War  materials to obstruct the sale of war  bonds or to utter any words supporting a  country at war with the U.S  these laws of course reflected  congress's desire to protect the  country's War efforts  they also reflected congress's efforts  to stifle what it believed were Radical  political and labor movements such as  socialism and anarchism  which still played a prominent role in  the country  labor unrest had continued into the 20th  century with the rise of groups such as  the industrial workers of the world  commonly known as The wobblies Who  announced that the struggle must go on  until the workers of the world organized  as a class take possession of the earth  and Machines of production and abolish  the wage system  the government pursued cases under the  Espionage Act vigorously  a famous example involved NASA's  publishing company which published the  masses a monthly political magazine that  described itself as revolutionary Frank  arrogant impertinent  the masses editor-in-chief was Max  Eastman who called himself the American  lyrical socialist a child of Walt  Whitman reared by Karl Marx  in 1917 the postmaster of New York City  informed the masses that the U.S mail  service would not distribute their  magazine  the Espionage Act included a  non-mailability provision that gave  broad authority to the post office  Department to exclude treasonable  materials from the mail  the postmaster had decided the masses  qualified as non-mailable  the masses sued  the case went before judge Learned Hand  a federal trial court judge in New York  and initially the masses won  as we've already seen Free Speech claims  usually lost in the courts but judge  hand granted the masses a preliminary  injunction against the government  effectively telling the postmaster he  had to continue shipping the magazine  hand did not actually decide the case  technically on First Amendment grounds  instead he construed the Espionage Act  so as to deny the postmaster the  authority to deem non-mailable the  magazine at issue in this case  nevertheless though he decided the case  on statutory rather than constitutional  grounds Han's opinion emphasized the  importance of freedom of speech in a  democracy  for example the Espionage Act gave the  government during wartime the authority  to censor a willfully false statement  judge hand held that the ACT did not  cover any of the material in the masses  magazine which is hand put it was within  the range of opinion and criticism  an opinion may be unpopular hand implied  but that is not the same thing as a  false statement  as a result hand believed the ACT did  not actually reach the material in  question  in another section of the opinion judge  hand addressed the government's effort  to censor material that it claimed  caused insubordination disloyalty Mutiny  or refusal of Duty in the armed forces  hand did not believe the relevant  portions of the masses had caused such  outcomes  criticism of a war is not the same thing  as causing troops to Mutiny or causing  civilians to dodge the draft  the government's aggressive reading of  the law had contended would allow the  suppression of all hostile criticism  this result would be untenable  democratic governments after all depend  on disagreement including disagreement  with the current Administration  and did acknowledge that the government  has some power to protect itself from  speech that threatens its very existence  hand however distinguished between  speech that criticizes existing policy  on the one hand and speech that is a  direct incitement of violent resistance  on the other  political agitation as he put it is a  safeguard of free government  political dissent then is  constitutionally protected speech  incitements to violently overthrow the  government by contrast can be punished  learn enhance insistence that  prosecutors even in times of War  could not go after a speaker simply for  criticizing the government was a bold  defense of free speech  it also fell on largely deaf ears  the court of appeals overturned Han's  opinion and lifted the injunction  as a result the post office could stop  the mailing of the masses  not long after the publication went out  of business  Learned Hand himself suffered some  repercussions too  hand was widely acknowledged as a star  of the lower Federal bench and many  considered him the leading candidate for  an open seat on the U.S court of appeals  for the second circuit the same court  that had reversed hands NASA's decision  hand did not get the promotion then  though several years later he would  had believed in some legal historians  agree that he was initially passed over  because his decision in the masses case  had ruffled too many feathers  indeed learned hands Progressive views  on freedom of speech were out of step  with most jurists of the day  in early 1919 a year and a half after  the masses case a tree with cases  arrived at the U.S Supreme Court  involving criminal convictions under the  Espionage Act  in each case the criminal defendants  asserted that their speech should be  constitutionally protected  in one case shank the United States  the defendants had been convicted of  obstructing military recruitment by  Distributing a flyer opposing the draft  one side said do not submit to  intimidation  the other side was headed assert your  rights it denied the power to send  citizens away to war and foreign lands  and asserted that if you do not assert  and support your rights you are helping  to deny or disparage rights which it is  the solemn duty of all citizens and  residents of the United States to retain  the second case for work B United States  involved a news reporter critical of the  U.S war policy  he was convicted of attempting to cause  disloyalty Mutiny and refusal of Duty in  the military  the third case Debs V United States was  probably the most extraordinary because  it involved a prominent political figure  Eugene Dabbs was the leader of the  Socialist Party and a frequent  presidential candidate  though Debs never really had a real  chance of winning the presidency he was  far from a fringe figure he was well  known throughout the country and in 1912  his presidential campaign had won about  six percent of the popular vote  ever since fighting had begun in Europe  dabs had argued against American  involvement in the war  in 1918 Debs gave a speech that attacked  capitalism and praised socialism  though debs's speech had not explicitly  instructed his audience to obstruct the  draft he had praised three members of  his party who were serving time for  their opposition to the draft  a zealous prosecutor went after Debs for  the speech and the jury convicted Debs  for violations of the Espionage Act  the U.S Supreme Court upheld all three  convictions  Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes's opinion  in the shank case explained the Court's  reasoning most fully  perhaps the same flyer would have been  acceptable in ordinary times Holmes  explained  but when a nation is at War many things  that might be said in time of Peace are  such a hindrance to its effort that  their utterance will not be endured so  long as men fight and that no court  could regard them as protected by any  constitutional right  incidentally by 1919 the fighting in  Europe had ended but the speech had  occurred during the war  that context mattered the question  Holmes said was whether the words given  the circumstances created a clear and  present danger  as Holmes put it in the opinions most  famous sentence  the most stringent protection of free  speech would not protect a man in  falsely shouting fire in a theater and  causing a panic  Holmes's views were typical of his  fellow jurists in fact in all three  cases the justices unanimously affirmed  the Espionage Act convictions and  brushed aside Free Speech concerns  as it turned out though Holmes himself  would eventually have second thoughts  the previous summer in June 1918 Holmes  had been on a train heading to a summer  home on the Massachusetts North Shore  Holmes had been sitting with his wife  but he got up to stretch his legs and  ran into an acquaintance  judge learned at hand the author of the  trial Court's masses opinion  hand was very well respected but Holmes  was three decades older and probably the  most prominent judge in the country  hand was nervous and the famous judge's  presence but he gathered the courage to  raise the topic of free speech  he apparently did not persuade Holmes  because a few days later he wrote Holmes  a letter Beginning by saying that he had  given up rather more easily than I now  feel disposed  had proceeded to explain in his letter  that Society should be tolerant of  dissenting views because we can never be  certain in any debate that we are  correct  if it turns out that the dissenters are  correct and the majority suppresses  their views then Society loses the  benefits of the dissenter's wisdom  Holmes was famously skeptical that there  was such a thing as objective truth and  Hans argument that we can never be  certain that we are correct was likely  crafted with his older colleague  skepticism in mind  Holmes loved intellectual sparring and  enthusiastically replied to Han's letter  but he remained unconvinced by Han's  arguments  Holmes argued that the majority has a  natural right to silence the other  fellow when he disagrees  he alluded to an earlier Supreme Court  case holding that an individual does not  have a right to opt out of a  governmentally mandated vaccination  campaign  Holmes generally believed that in a  democracy the majority can craft  whatever laws it chooses  free speech he wrote to hand stands no  differently than freedom from  vaccination  the two men continued to correspond but  Holmes's views on Free Speech appeared  unmoved  when the Supreme Court in early 1919  released its opinions and shank for work  and Debs judge learn at hand must have  grieved  not only had every Justice rejected the  free speech claim in each case but  Holmes himself had penned each opinion  despite his best efforts judge hand had  failed to persuade Justice Holmes  after the Deb's decision though hand  tried again writing to Holmes to suggest  that instead of Permitting the  conviction of a man because a judge  deemed it likely that his speech will  obstruct the draft a judge should  instead focus on what the speaker has  actually said  hand didn't say so in his letter but it  wasn't clear that Debs had instructed  his followers to obstruct the draft  and also seemed worried about leaving  such cases up to juries since juries  likely will have the same prejudices as  the majority that enacted laws like the  Espionage Act in the first place  freedom of speech hand implied had to  accomplish something more than that  Hans new letter apparently didn't have  an immediate impact on Holmes who was  very busy  Hovis was brilliant but his reply  suggests that he hadn't taken the time  to think through hands ideas carefully  but hand wasn't the only important legal  thinker dismayed by the Court's failure  to protect free speech  Ernst freund a prominent law professor  at the University of Chicago published  an article criticizing the Court's  decision in the Deb's case  freund wrote  to be permitted to agitate at your own  Peril subject to a jury's guessing at  motive tendency and possible effect  makes the right of free speech a  precarious gift  Holmes had an active mind and some  Scholars believed that these and related  criticisms prompted him to reconsider  his views  in late 1919 another Free Speech case  arrived at the court Abrams V United  States  a group of Russian immigrants who were  sympathetic to the recent Bolshevik  Revolution in their home country had  distributed circulars in New York City  opposing what they perceived to be the  U.S government's efforts to crush the  revolution  the Russians advocated a general strike  and appeal to workers in ammunition  factories to stop producing weapons that  might be used against the Russian  revolutionaries  the U.S government charged the Russians  with violating provisions of the  Espionage Act prohibiting speech that  advocated halting the production of  ammunition necessary to prosecute the  war  the defendants were convicted but  eventually took their case to the U.S  Supreme Court arguing that the  convictions violated their free speech  rights  once again as in shank frowwork and Debs  the court upheld the convictions thus  rejecting the Free Speech claims  however this time Justice Holmes joined  by Justice Louis Brandeis dissented  in Abrams Holmes seem to adopt hands  view that the government should be  forced to prove more than merely the  fact that this speech might produce  unwanted Behavior  it should not be enough Holmes argued  that speech has a bad tendency to incite  illegal activity  rather the test should be whether there  is a present danger of immediate evil  this immediacy requirement protects more  speech because it is difficult for  prosecutors to prove that a speech will  bring about immediate harm  Holmes concluded his dissent with a  stirring defense of free speech  the paragraph begins curiously by  offering an argument that seems to favor  censorship  Holmes lays out this argument but then  pivots and explains why Society should  protect even distasteful speech  this passage is so great and so famous  I'm going to read a significant excerpt  Holmes wrote  persecution for the expression of  opinions seems to me perfectly logical  if you have no doubt of your promises or  your power and want a certain result  with all your heart you naturally  express your wishes in law and sweep  away all opposition  to allow opposition by speech seems to  indicate that you think the speech  impotent as when a man says that he has  squared the circle  or that you do not care wholeheartedly  for the result  or that you doubt either your power or  your premises  but when men have realized that time has  upset many fighting figs they may come  to believe even more than they believe  the very foundations of their own  conduct that the ultimate good desired  is better reached by free trade in ideas  that the best test of Truth is the power  of the thought to get itself accepted in  the competition of the market  and that truth is the only ground upon  which their wishes safely can be carried  out  that at any rate is the theory of our  constitution  it is an experiment as all life is an  experiment  every year if not every day we have to  wager our Salvation upon some prophecy  based upon imperfect knowledge  while that experiment is part of our  system  I think that we should be eternally  Vigilant against attempts to check the  expression of opinions that we loathe  and believed to be fraught with death  unless they so imminently threaten  immediate interference with the lawful  and pressing purposes of the law  that an immediate check is required to  save the country  its natural Holmes tells us to want to  silence one's opponents  but we can never be sure we are right  and if Society does not permit a free  exchange of ideas  will never have the chance to weigh  ideas against each other  only through the free trade and ideas  can we know which ideas are the best  Holmes's dissent is one of the most  famous opinions in Supreme Court history  it demonstrates how a Justice even a  brilliant one can change his mind in  just a few short months  more importantly The Descent paved the  way for future Supreme Court decisions  that protected speech  today free speech is a core  constitutional value it's one of the few  constitutional issues that liberals and  conservatives often agree upon  of course free speech is not an absolute  right because no right is absolute  there are cases when the government can  punish speech  such as immediate incitements to  violence or true threats or child  pornography  most people agree that speech of this  sort should not be tolerated because the  harms it brings about are so great  and there are cases where Society still  debates whether government should be  permitted to punish speech  such as disinformation that can erode  our democracy  with just a handful of exceptions though  the court today protects Free Speech  vigorously  and we can trace that doctrine's roots  to Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes's  dissent in Abrams  and perhaps even his fortuitous meeting  on a train with judge learn at hand  one lesson of this story is the  Constitutional Provisions can take on  new meaning in New Times  Americans today think of freedom of  speech as foundational  but it has played a significant role in  Supreme Court Doctrine for less than a  century  another observation is that the ultimate  Vindication of free speech helped lead  to other important constitutional  changes  protest movements on a wide range of  issues  civil rights women's rights gay rights  gun rights all relied on the country's  Free Speech protections to make their  points vigorously  those movements in various ways helped  reshape the country's constitutional  commitments  speech thus is truly a core right  it is a right that helps protect other  rights
 

